<section class="boundaries" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
  <div class="boundaries__paper paper-fold">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="boundaries-title">Privacy without a fairy tale</h2>
      <p>
        The design limits who can read a letter and what strangers learn about
        each other. It does not pretend the service is invisible.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="boundaries__columns">
      <div>
        <h3>What is protected</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>PostgreSQL stores application-encrypted correspondence.</li>
          <li>Only the matched participants can request an ordinary message.</li>
          <li>Aliases are non-searchable and reveal no profile or history.</li>
          <li>This static website has no letter API, account state, or analytics.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What remains visible</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Orifude is not end-to-end encrypted.</li>
          <li>The authorized post office can decrypt ordinary messages.</li>
          <li>The service retains operational metadata and participant relationships.</li>
          <li>A compromised running service is outside storage-encryption protection.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <aside class="safety" aria-labelledby="safety-title">
    <h2 id="safety-title">Report, burn, and block</h2>
    <p>
      A recipient can report a letter or reply using a fixed reason. Reporting
      hides the exchange, stores separately encrypted evidence for restricted
      moderation, and permanently blocks future matching.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other person is not told who blocked them. A lost-device revocation
      credential can delete an identity, but it cannot read letters or create a
      session.
    </p>
  </aside>
</section>
